    "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n               <jats:p>We report the discovery of high-energy <jats:italic>\u03b3</jats:italic>-ray emission in the vicinity of G213.0-0.6, which is debated as a supernova remnant (SNR) or an ionized hydrogen (H<jats:sub>II</jats:sub>) region. Using 16 yr Pass 8 data from Fermi Large Area Telescope, we found three extended sources with different photon spectra in this region, which we label as SrcA, SrcB, and SrcC. Among them, the <jats:italic>\u03b3</jats:italic>-ray source SrcA with a log-parabola spectrum is spatially coincident with a star-forming region and several OB stars. The power-law spectra source SrcB is spatially coincident with an SNR radio shell. SrcC, with a harder power-law photon spectrum, is located outside of the radio shell structure. All of them are spatially coincident with a dense molecular cloud (MC) in the velocity range of 35\u201354 km s<jats:sup>\u22121</jats:sup>. In this scenario, SrcB can be interpreted as the GeV counterpart of the SNR, and its <jats:italic>\u03b3</jats:italic>-ray emission originates from the shock\u2013cloud interaction. SrcA and SrcC originate from the escaped CRs illuminating a nearby MC. For SrcA, another possibility is that the <jats:italic>\u03b3</jats:italic>-ray emission originates from a young stellar cluster associated with a star-forming region; however, the supporting evidence remains insufficient to draw a definitive conclusion.</jats:p>",
